<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00172</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.7.3: Understanding Equations with No Solution or Infinitely Many</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cbbaf39a-7f4e-4c89-8ebf-1ec40366f4a4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Noah is having trouble solving two equations. In each case, he took steps that he thought were acceptable but ended up with statements that are clearly not true.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">Analyze Noah’s work on each equation and the moves he made. Were they acceptable moves? Why do you think he ended up with a false equation?</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Discuss your observations with your group and be prepared to share your conclusions. If you get stuck, consider solving each equation.</para>
      <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation. \( x+6 =4x+1-3x \)</para>
      <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Commutative Property. \( x+6 =4x-3x+1 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms. \( x+6 =x+1 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Subtract \(x\) from each side. \( 6 =1 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation. \( 2(5+x)-1=3x+9 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Distributive Property. \( 10+2x-1 =3x+9 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Subtract 10 from each side.  \( 2x-1 =3x-1 \)</para>
      <para id="para-00013"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Add 1 to each side. \( 2x=3x \)</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00014">Why did Noah have no solution for either equation?</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Enter your answer:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">Your answer may vary, but here is an example: All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that means there is no value of \(x\) that makes the first equation true. We can see it in the third line: \(x+6=x+1\). There is no number that can make the equation true.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00018">Why did Noah arrive at no solution for the equation solved in Example 2?</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Enter your answer:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Example 1: All the moves that Noah made were acceptable, but because he ended up with a false statement, that means there is no value of \( x \) that makes the first equation true. We can see it in the third line: \( x + 6 = x + 1 \). There is no number that can make the equation true.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">Example 2: Noah's moves seem acceptable except for the last one. If he had subtracted \( 2x \) from each side of the equation, he would have \( 0 = x \). Instead, he divided both sides by \( x \) which would give an undefined number if \( x\;\;is\;\;0 \).</para>
          <para id="para-00024">Noah tried to solve a third equation and ended up with a true statement, without variables.</para>
          <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
          <para id="para-00026">\( 2 (4 + x) - 2 = 2x + 6 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00027"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Apply the Distributive Property.</para>
          <para id="para-00028">\( 8+ 2x - 2 =2x + 6 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00029"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine Like Terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00030">\( 2x +6 = 2x +6 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Subtract 6 from both sides.   </para>
          <para id="para-00032">\( 2x = 2x \)</para>
          <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Subtract \(2x\) from both sides.</para>
          <para id="para-00034">\( 0=0 \)</para>
          <para id="para-00035">Since there is not a variable and the statement is true, any value of \(x\) would be a solution for the original equation. So, this equation has infinitely many solutions.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00036">We cannot divide the number 100 by zero because dividing by zero is undefined. Instead, try dividing 100 by 10, then 1, then 0.1, then 0.01. What do you notice happens as you divide by smaller numbers?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00037">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00038">10, 100, 1000, 10,000. For example: The quotients are getting very large.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00039">Now try dividing the number -100 by 10, by 1, by 0.1, 0.01. What is the same and what is different when you compare to question 1?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00041">-10, -100, -1000, -10,000. For example: The absolute values of the quotients are the same, but the sign is opposite..</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00005">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00042">In middle school, you used tape diagrams to represent division. This tape diagram shows that 6÷2=3</para>
            
              <media alt="Tape diagram, 3 parts, each marked 2.">
                <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_09_06_01.png"/>
              </media>
            
            <para id="para-00043">Draw a tape diagram that shows why  \(6\div\frac{1}{2}=12\).</para>
            <para id="para-00044">After you have drawn your answer, select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00045">
              
                <media alt="A tape diagram showing twelve groups of 1/2">
                  <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_09_06_02.png"/>
                </media>
              
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00046">Try to draw a tape diagram that represents 6÷0. Explain why this is so difficult.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00048">The zero takes up no space, so no matter how many copies we have it could never reach 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00049">Which of the following equations has a solution?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Equations Without Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00050">What happens when we solve \( 5z = 5z - 1\)?</para>
        <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Subtract \(5z\) to get the \(z\) terms together on one side.</para>
        <para id="para-00052">\(\begin{aligned}5z-5z &amp;=5z-5z-1\end{aligned}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify and the \(z\) terms are gone!.</para>
        <para id="para-00054">\(\begin{aligned}0 &amp;=1\end{aligned}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00055">Since 0 does not equal \(-1\), this equation led to a false statement. There are no solutions to this equation.</para>
        <para id="para-00056"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00057">Solve \( 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) \).</para>
        <para id="para-00058"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like terms. \( 5m+27+9m=14m−22 \) \(14m+27=14m−22\)</para>
        <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. \(14m−14m+27=14m−14m−22\) \(27=-22\)</para>
        <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution. The equation \(27=−22\) is NOT a true statement. The equation 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) has no solutions.</para>
      </section>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
        <para id="para-00061">What if an equation results in a true statement without variables?</para>
        <para id="para-00062">The equations \( -4 = -4 \), \( 5 = 5 \), and \( 0 = 0 \) are all true statements because one side is equal to the other. This means that any value of the variable in the original equation would be a solution. When this happens in the final step of an equation, there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
        <para id="para-00063"><emphasis>Example:</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00064">Solve \( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
        <para id="para-00065"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible. Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses. Combine like terms.</para>
        <para id="para-00066">\( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
        <para id="para-00067"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. \( 2y−2y+6=2y−2y+6\) \(6=6\)</para>
        <para id="para-00068">\(2y+6=2y+6\)</para>
        <para id="para-00069"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check the solution. We say the solution to the equation is all of the real numbers.An equation that is true for any value of the variable is called an <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">identity</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00070">The solution of an identity is all real numbers.</para>
      </section>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Equations with No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions </title>
          <exercise id="exer-00007">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00071">Solve \( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)and determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00073">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00074">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00075"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00076">\( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)</para>
            <para id="para-00077"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00078">\( 10x - 4 =10x - 4 \)</para>
            <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00080">\(\begin{aligned}10x - 10x - 4 &amp;=10x - 10x - 4 \\-4 &amp;=-4 \end{aligned}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00081"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00082">This is a true statement so there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

          <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00083">Solve \( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \) and determine the number of solutions.</para>
            <para id="para-00084">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00085">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
            <para id="para-00087"><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Original equation.</para>
            <para id="para-00088">\( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \)</para>
            <para id="para-00089"><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Distribute</para>
            <para id="para-00090">\( 3x - 4 = 3x -6 \)</para>
            <para id="para-00091"><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
            <para id="para-00092">\(\begin{aligned}3x - 3x - 4 &amp;=3x - 3x - 6 \\-4 &amp;=-6 \end{aligned}\)</para>
            <para id="para-00093"><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check the solution</para>
            <para id="para-00094">The equation \( -4=-6\) is NOT a true statement. The equation \(3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \) has no solutions.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
